<section xml:id="sec-mod5-practice-set">
  <title>Module 5: Practice Problem Set</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 100 minutes</p>

    <p>
      Problems are labeled <m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> (Foundational),
      <m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> (Standard), and <m>[\mathbf{C}]</m>
      (Challenge). Work in order within each level; later problems
      in a level often build on earlier ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      These problems are not submitted, but you should write out
      complete solutions. For any problem that asks you to
      <em>prove</em> something, apply the standards from the Worked
      Examples: strategy stated up front, complete sentences, every
      step justified, and the test or theorem invoked by name.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       FOUNDATIONAL
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod5-ps-f">
    <title>Foundational Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems practice the basic moves of the module: computing
      partial sums by hand, deciding whether geometric series converge and
      finding their sums, applying the Divergence Test, and determining
      convergence or divergence with the Direct Comparison Test.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-f1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Partial Sums by Hand</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each series, write out <m>S_1, S_2, S_3, S_4</m> as
        fractions. Then guess (no proof required) whether the series
        converges and, if so, to what.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{3^{k-1}}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k(k+1)}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (-1)^{k+1}</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>S_1 = 1</m>, <m>S_2 = 4/3</m>, <m>S_3 = 13/9</m>,
        <m>S_4 = 40/27</m>; approaches <m>3/2</m> (geometric, ratio <m>1/3</m>).
        (b) <m>S_1 = 1/2</m>, <m>S_2 = 2/3</m>, <m>S_3 = 3/4</m>,
        <m>S_4 = 4/5</m>; approaches 1 (telescoping, cf. Worked
        Example 1).
        (c) <m>S_1 = 1</m>, <m>S_2 = 0</m>, <m>S_3 = 1</m>,
        <m>S_4 = 0</m>; oscillates, diverges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-f2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Geometric Series</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each series, determine whether it converges, and if so
        compute its sum. Identify the first term <m>a</m> and common
        ratio <m>r</m>, then apply the geometric-series formula
        <m>a/(1 - r)</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} 3 \cdot \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^{k}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{k}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right)^{k}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{5}{3}\right)^{k}</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>a = 3</m>, <m>r = 1/4</m>; sum <m>= 3/(1 - 1/4) = 4</m>.
        (b) First term <m>2/3</m>, ratio <m>2/3</m>;
        sum <m>= (2/3)/(1 - 2/3) = 2</m>.
        (c) <m>a = 1</m>, <m>r = -1/2</m>; sum <m>= 1/(1 - (-1/2)) = 2/3</m>.
        (d) <m>|r| = 5/3 \gt 1</m>, so <m>(5/3)^k \geq 1</m> for every
        <m>k</m> and the terms do not tend to 0; diverges by the
        Divergence Test.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-f3">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> The Divergence Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Divergence Test to show that each of the following
        series diverges, or state that the test is inconclusive.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{k}{k + 1}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} (-1)^{k+1} \frac{k}{k + 1}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \cos\!\left(\frac{1}{k}\right)</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>a_k = k/(k+1) \to 1 \neq 0</m>; diverges.
        (b) <m>a_k = 1/k \to 0</m>; Divergence Test is inconclusive.
        (The harmonic series <em>does</em> diverge, but by a
        different argument.)
        (c) <m>|a_k| = k/(k+1) \to 1</m>, so <m>a_k \not\to 0</m>
        (the terms oscillate between values close to <m>\pm 1</m>);
        diverges.
        (d) <m>\cos(1/k) \to \cos(0) = 1 \neq 0</m>, because
        <m>1/k \to 0</m> and cosine is continuous at 0 (a calculus
        fact for now; continuity is the subject of
        <xref ref="ch-mod6" text="custom">Module 6</xref>); diverges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-f4">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Direct Comparison</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Direct Comparison Test to determine convergence or
        divergence of each series. Identify the <q>known</q> series
        you are comparing to.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k^2 + 3}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{2}{\sqrt{k}}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^k + k}</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>1/(k^2 + 3) \leq 1/k^2</m>, and <m>\sum 1/k^2</m>
        converges; so <m>\sum 1/(k^2 + 3)</m> converges.
        (b) <m>2/\sqrt{k} \geq 2/k</m> for <m>k \geq 1</m>
        (since <m>\sqrt{k} \leq k</m>); <m>\sum 2/k</m> diverges;
        so <m>\sum 2/\sqrt{k}</m> diverges.
        (c) <m>1/(2^k + k) \leq 1/2^k</m>; <m>\sum 1/2^k</m>
        converges (geometric); so <m>\sum 1/(2^k + k)</m> converges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       STANDARD
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod5-ps-s">
    <title>Standard Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems apply the same moves to longer arguments: a
      telescoping proof from the definition of series convergence,
      applications of the Ratio Test and the Limit Comparison Test, a proof
      using the Alternating Series Test with a classification as absolute
      or conditional, and a pair of examples showing that the Divergence
      Test is inconclusive when <m>a_k \to 0</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-s1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> A Telescoping Proof</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove directly from the definition of series convergence that
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{(2k - 1)(2k + 1)} = \frac{1}{2}.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must follow the template of Worked Example 1:
        (i) identify a partial-fraction decomposition of the summand;
        (ii) find a closed form for the partial sum <m>S_n</m> by
        telescoping; (iii) take the limit using the Algebra of Limits.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Verify that
        <md>\frac{1}{(2k - 1)(2k + 1)} = \frac{1}{2} \left(\frac{1}{2k - 1} - \frac{1}{2k + 1}\right).</md>
        Then <m>S_n = \frac{1}{2}\left(1 - \frac{1}{2n + 1}\right)</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-s2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Ratio Test Applications</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Ratio Test to determine convergence or divergence of
        each series. Show the ratio computation explicitly.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{k}{2^k}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{3^k}{k!}</m></li>
          <li><m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{k^2}{k!}</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Ratio <m>= (k+1)/2^{k+1} \cdot 2^k/k = (k+1)/(2k) \to 1/2 \lt 1</m>;
        converges.
        (b) Ratio <m>= 3^{k+1}/(k+1)! \cdot k!/3^k = 3/(k+1) \to 0 \lt 1</m>;
        converges.
        (c) Ratio <m>= (k+1)^2/(k+1)! \cdot k!/k^2 = (k+1)/k^2 \to 0 \lt 1</m>;
        converges.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-s3">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Limit Comparison</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Limit Comparison Test to determine convergence or
        divergence of
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{3k^2 + 1}{k^4 + 7k + 2}.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Identify your comparison series, compute the ratio of terms,
        evaluate its limit, and invoke the test by name.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Leading-order behavior is <m>3k^2/k^4 = 3/k^2</m>; compare
        with <m>\sum 1/k^2</m>. The ratio limit is 3.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-s4">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> An Alternating Series</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{k+1}}{k^2 + 1}</md>
        converges. Does it converge absolutely or conditionally?
        Justify your answer in full.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof should (i) verify both hypotheses of the
        Alternating Series Test; (ii) separately investigate absolute
        convergence by testing <m>\sum |a_k|</m>; (iii) conclude with
        a classification.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For AST: <m>b_k = 1/(k^2 + 1)</m> is positive, decreasing
        (since <m>k^2 + 1 \lt (k+1)^2 + 1</m>), and tends to 0. For
        absolute: <m>\sum 1/(k^2 + 1)</m> converges by direct
        comparison with <m>\sum 1/k^2</m>. Hence the series converges
        absolutely.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-s5">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> The Divergence Test is One-Way</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Give one example of a series <m>\sum a_k</m> with
        <m>a_k \to 0</m> that <em>converges</em>, and one example with
        <m>a_k \to 0</m> that <em>diverges</em>. For each, cite the
        reason for convergence or divergence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use this pair of examples to explain, in your own words,
        why the Divergence Test has the one-way structure
        <q>if <m>a_k \not\to 0</m>, then <m>\sum a_k</m> diverges</q>
        and not the stronger two-way statement.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Convergent with terms <m>\to 0</m>: <m>\sum 1/k^2</m>
        (<m>p</m>-series, <m>p = 2 \gt 1</m>).
        Divergent with terms <m>\to 0</m>: <m>\sum 1/k</m>
        (harmonic; see <xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-25-4"/>).
        The pair shows that <m>a_k \to 0</m> is <em>necessary</em>
        (because convergent sequences of partial sums force their
        differences to 0) but not <em>sufficient</em>: for a series of
        nonnegative terms, the partial sums increase, the rate at
        which <m>a_k \to 0</m> determines whether they stay bounded,
        and for <m>1/k</m> they do not.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       CHALLENGE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod5-ps-c">
    <title>Challenge Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems extend the module's ideas: two proofs that absolute
      convergence implies convergence, one using the Cauchy criterion
      and one using the Comparison Test, and a
      comparison of the Ratio and Root Tests on a series where the Ratio
      Test is inconclusive.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-c1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Absolute Convergence Implies Convergence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove: if <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} |a_k|</m> converges, then
        <m>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k</m> converges.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must use the Cauchy criterion for sequences
        (Module 4). Let <m>T_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} |a_k|</m> and
        <m>S_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k</m>. Use that <m>(T_n)</m> is
        Cauchy (it converges by hypothesis) together with the
        triangle inequality on finite sums to show <m>(S_n)</m> is
        Cauchy, and invoke Cauchy completeness of <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This first proof writes out in full the outline of
        <xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-26-4"/>. Then give a second proof that
        does not use the Cauchy criterion, using the Comparison Test
        and the algebra of convergent series
        (<xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-25-5"/>) instead. Finally, for each
        proof, name the <xref ref="ch-mod4" text="custom">Module 4</xref>
        theorem through which the completeness of <m>\R</m> enters.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. Since <m>(T_n)</m> converges,
        it is Cauchy: there is <m>N</m> with <m>|T_n - T_m| \lt \varepsilon</m>
        for <m>n \gt m \geq N</m>. Note <m>T_n - T_m = \sum_{k = m+1}^{n} |a_k|</m>.
        Then
        <md>|S_n - S_m| = \left|\sum_{k=m+1}^{n} a_k\right|
        \leq \sum_{k=m+1}^{n} |a_k| = T_n - T_m \lt \varepsilon.</md>
        Hence <m>(S_n)</m> is Cauchy, hence convergent in <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the second proof: <m>0 \leq a_k + |a_k| \leq 2|a_k|</m> for
        every <m>k</m>, and <m>\sum 2|a_k|</m> converges, so
        <m>\sum (a_k + |a_k|)</m> converges by the Comparison Test.
        Now write <m>a_k = (a_k + |a_k|) - |a_k|</m>. Completeness
        enters the first proof through Cauchy completeness of
        <m>\R</m>, and the second through the Monotone Convergence
        Theorem, on which the proof of the Comparison Test rests.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod5-ps-c2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Ratio Test versus Root Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the series
        <md>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k, \qquad
        a_k = \begin{cases}
          2^{-k}, \amp k \text{ even}, \\
          3^{-k}, \amp k \text{ odd}.
        \end{cases}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Compute <m>a_{k+1}/a_k</m> separately for <m>k</m> odd and
            for <m>k</m> even. Does <m>\lim_{k \to \infty} a_{k+1}/a_k</m>
            exist? What does the Ratio Test
            (<xref ref="ex-mod5-sg-26-3"/>) tell you?
          </li>
          <li>
            Compute <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k}</m> separately for <m>k</m> even
            and for <m>k</m> odd. Does
            <m>\lim_{k \to \infty} \sqrt[k]{a_k}</m> exist? Use the
            eventual-bound form of the Root Test (in
            <xref ref="par-mod5-sg-26-root" text="custom">the Study Guide</xref>)
            to decide whether the
            series converges.
          </li>
          <li>
            What does this example show about the Ratio Test and the
            two forms of the Root Test? Could the Comparison Test have
            settled the question directly?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        (a) The ratio <m>a_{k+1}/a_k</m> oscillates: for <m>k</m> odd,
        <m>a_{k+1}/a_k = 2^{-(k+1)}/3^{-k} = 3^k/2^{k+1}</m>, which
        <em>grows without bound</em>; for <m>k</m> even,
        <m>a_{k+1}/a_k = 3^{-(k+1)}/2^{-k} = 2^k/3^{k+1}</m>, which
        tends to 0. The limit does not exist, so the Ratio Test, which
        assumes the limit exists, gives no conclusion.
        (b) For <m>k</m> even, <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} = \sqrt[k]{2^{-k}} = 1/2</m>;
        for <m>k</m> odd, <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} = 1/3</m>. The even-indexed
        and odd-indexed roots have different limits, so
        <m>\lim \sqrt[k]{a_k}</m> does not exist and the limit form of
        the Root Test does not apply. But <m>\sqrt[k]{a_k} \leq 1/2</m>
        for every <m>k</m>, so the eventual-bound form with
        <m>r = 1/2</m> shows that the series converges.
        (c) Neither test in limit form applies; the eventual-bound form
        of the Root Test decides the question. Nor would an eventual
        bound on the ratios help, though that is what the proof of the
        Ratio Test actually uses: for every odd <m>k \geq 3</m> the ratio
        <m>3^k/2^{k+1}</m> is greater than 1, so no <m>r \lt 1</m>
        bounds the ratios for all large <m>k</m>. The ratios compare
        neighboring terms, which here differ wildly in size; the roots
        compare each <m>a_k</m> with <m>r^k</m> directly, and every
        <m>a_k</m> is at most <m>2^{-k}</m>. That same inequality
        (<m>3^{-k} \leq 2^{-k}</m>) also settles the question by direct
        comparison with the geometric series <m>\sum 2^{-k}</m>, so the
        Root Test is not the only route here.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

</section>
